                <p>Few labels in watchmaking cause as much confusion as “Made in USA.” The US and Swiss rules measure different things, so a watch that's mostly made in America can be barred from saying so, while a watch with many imported parts can still be Swiss Made. Several writers have covered this well, and we link to them below rather than repeat their work.</p>

                <h2>How the directory handles it</h2>
                <p>We don't rate or verify how American a watch is. We're volunteers, and we can't inspect factories or supply chains. Instead:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>A listing means a business is <strong>based in America</strong>, not that its products are made here.</li>
                    <li>Where a brand describes where its products are designed, made or assembled, we report it as the <strong>brand's own claim</strong> (“the brand says…”), with a link to where it says so when we have one.</li>
                    <li>We don't repeat other people's claims about a brand as fact.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>If a listing gets a brand's claims wrong, or a brand's claims have changed, please <a href="/update/">tell us</a>. The reasoning behind this approach is in the <a href="/changelog/#only-brand-claims">changelog</a>.</p>

                <h2>The US rule: “all or virtually all”</h2>
                <p>In the United States, the Federal Trade Commission (FTC) sets the standard. An unqualified “Made in USA” claim means the product is “all or virtually all” made in the US: its significant parts, processing and labor should be of US origin, with no more than negligible foreign content. It isn't enough for a product to be designed or assembled here, or for most of its value to come from here. Since 2021 the standard has also been a formal rule for labels.</p>
                <p>Products that don't meet that bar can still make a <strong>qualified claim</strong>, as long as it's true and the brand can back it up. That's why you'll see wording like “Made in USA with a Swiss movement,” “Assembled in USA” or “Designed in USA.”</p>
                <p>For a watch, the bar is especially high. A handful of parts, such as jewels, mainsprings and hairsprings, are rarely made in the US today, so even watches whose plates, bridges, wheels and cases are cut here may not qualify for an unqualified “Made in USA.”</p>

                <h2>The Swiss rule: 60% of the cost</h2>
                <p>Switzerland's rule for watches, in force since 2017, works differently. A watch can be labeled Swiss Made if it has a Swiss movement, is cased up and finally inspected in Switzerland, at least 60% of its manufacturing costs arise in Switzerland, and its technical development takes place there. The movement has its own test, which includes being assembled and inspected in Switzerland and a minimum share of Swiss parts by value.</p>
                <p>So a Swiss Made watch can contain a case, dial, hands, strap or other parts made elsewhere, as long as it meets the cost threshold and the Swiss steps happen in Switzerland.</p>

                <h2>Why the two are hard to compare</h2>
                <p>The US rule asks whether nearly every part is American; the Swiss rule asks whether enough of the cost is Swiss, and whether key steps happen in Switzerland. A US watch and a Swiss watch could have the same share of domestic work and end up with very different labels.</p>
                <p>That has a practical effect for anyone reading claims. A US brand that names its Swiss or Japanese movement is giving you more detail, not admitting to less. Being specific about imported parts doesn't make a watch less American than one whose maker says nothing at all. At the same time, nobody checks most claims in advance, and some brands use flags and “American” branding more loosely than the rules allow.</p>
                <p>Writers who cover this in more depth:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>In <a href="https://www.screwdowncrown.com/p/1776-atelier-hand-finishing-american-watchmaking" target="_blank" rel="noopener">“There Is No Fair”</a> (ScrewDownCrown, July 2026), kingflum compares the US, Swiss, Canadian and European rules through the example of a Texas brand whose movement is mostly cut from American raw stock but uses Swiss-made jewels and mainsprings. As the brand's co-founder Jason Lu puts it there, “It is not enough that the watch is assembled here.”</li>
                    <li>In <a href="https://quillandpad.com/2019/05/11/modern-american-watches-the-truth-behind-the-dial/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">“Modern American Watches: The Truth Behind the Dial”</a> (Quill &amp; Pad, May 2019), Derek Weinberg sets out both rules side by side and looks at how several American brands have labeled their watches.</li>
                    <li>In <a href="https://www.gearpatrol.com/watches/a263869/what-makes-a-watch-american-made/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">“What Makes a Watch ‘American-Made’?”</a> (Gear Patrol, 2016, updated 2021), Andrew Connor explains why American makers struggle to meet the US standard after the domestic supply base disappeared.</li>
                    <li>In <a href="https://www.insidehook.com/article/watches/10-best-american-watch-companies" target="_blank" rel="noopener">“On a Definition of ‘American-Made’ Watches”</a> (InsideHook, April 2018), Walker Loetscher argues for a looser working definition built around American hands doing real production work.</li>
                </ul>

                <h2>Reading a brand's claims</h2>
                <p>Some common wording, and what it usually means under the US rules. It's a guide, not a verdict on any brand.</p>
                <dl class="claims">
                    <dt>“Made in USA,” “American made,” “Built in USA”</dt>
                    <dd>Unqualified claims. Under FTC rules they mean all or virtually all of the product is made here.</dd>
                    <dt>“Made in USA with imported parts” or “…with a Swiss movement”</dt>
                    <dd>Qualified claims. Much of the work is done here, and the brand names what isn't.</dd>
                    <dt>“Assembled in USA”</dt>
                    <dd>The watch is put together here, from parts that may come from anywhere.</dd>
                    <dt>“Designed in USA” or a US address on the dial</dt>
                    <dd>The brand is American; this says nothing on its own about where the watch is made.</dd>
                    <dt>Percentages, such as “more than 90% made in-house”</dt>
                    <dd>The brand's own measure. Look for what the percentage counts (parts, cost or labor) and what's left out.</dd>
                    <dt>Claims about one part, such as “American-made case” or “US-assembled movement”</dt>
                    <dd>True of that part only, if accurate; other parts may be made elsewhere.</dd>
                </dl>
                <p>When a claim matters to you, the best source is the brand itself: most will tell you where each part comes from if you ask.</p>

